{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And she said, Here is my maidservant, etc.</b> She was precise in saying <b>my maidservant</b> before mentioning his coming to her, and she did not say ״come to my maidservant״ as Sarah said, as I explained (Genesis 16:5). She intended to say that after he comes to her she would no longer be called her maidservant. And therefore Scripture said afterward ״וַתִּתֵּן לוֹ בִּלְהָה שִׁפְחָתָהּ לְאִשָּׁה״ — she has the status of a wife to him, and her children are freeborn. And likewise Leah did, for she gave her to him as a wife, and the name \"maidservant\" was uprooted from Zilpah." "<b>And what</b> we find regarding Sarah (Genesis 16:3), that she too mentioned the name of wifehood upon Hagar — that was a breach on her part, in that she was precise to say the word ״לוֹ״ (\"to him\"), and see what I wrote there, that Rachel did not say thus." "<b>And what</b> you will find, that Scripture mentions afterward (Genesis 30:7) ״Bilhah, Rachel's maidservant,״ etc. — the meaning is that she had been Rachel's maidservant. Scripture there also intends to mention the praise of the righteousness of the Matriarchs in giving their maidservants to their husbands. And Scripture did not concern itself with error, since the matter is already clarified in the preceding verses; and see what I wrote in parashat Vayeshev (Genesis 37:2)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:03.926890824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}